@startuml Decorator-Middleware-Variations
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title Three ways to write the same pattern — all three build the same chain

package "1. classic — a class per concern" {
  interface PriceFeed
  abstract class PriceFeedDecorator {
    - inner : PriceFeed
  }
  class "LoggingPriceFeed\nTimingPriceFeed\nRetryingPriceFeed\nCachingPriceFeed\nRateLimitingPriceFeed" as Concretes

  PriceFeed <|.. PriceFeedDecorator
  PriceFeedDecorator o--> PriceFeed
  PriceFeedDecorator <|-- Concretes
}
note bottom of Concretes
  new TimingPriceFeed(
    new LoggingPriceFeed(
      new CachingPriceFeed(
        new RetryingPriceFeed(feed, 3),
        clock, ttl),
      log, "orders"),
    clock, metrics)

  Correct, and reads inside-out.
  Best when a concern has real state,
  invariants, and tests of its own.
end note

package "2. functional — a function per concern" {
  interface PriceFeedMiddleware <<UnaryOperator<PriceFeed>>> {
    + apply(next : PriceFeed) : PriceFeed
    --
    {static} logging(log, name)
    {static} timing(clock, metrics)
    {static} retrying(maxAttempts)
    {static} caching(clock, ttl)
    {static} apply(base, middleware...)
  }
}
note bottom of PriceFeedMiddleware
  PriceFeedMiddleware.apply(feed,
    timing(clock, metrics),   // outermost
    logging(log, "orders"),
    caching(clock, ttl),
    retrying(3));             // innermost

  No class per concern; state lives in
  the closure. A decorator is literally
  a PriceFeed -> PriceFeed function.
  In Java a lambda IS a PriceFeed;
  C# needs a 3-line DelegatePriceFeed.
end note

package "3. fluent — the same classes, assembled in reading order" {
  class PriceFeedPipeline {
    {static} around(base : PriceFeed) : PriceFeedPipeline
    + withLogging(log, name) : PriceFeedPipeline
    + withTiming(clock, metrics) : PriceFeedPipeline
    + withRetry(maxAttempts) : PriceFeedPipeline
    + withCache(clock, ttl) : PriceFeedPipeline
    + withRateLimit(clock, limit, window) : PriceFeedPipeline
    + build() : PriceFeed
  }
  PriceFeedPipeline ..> Concretes : assembles
}
note bottom of PriceFeedPipeline
  PriceFeedPipeline.around(feed)
    .withTiming(clock, metrics)
    .withLogging(log, "orders")
    .withCache(clock, ttl)
    .withRetry(3)
    .build();

  **First listed = outermost**, so the code
  reads in the direction a request travels
  (the ASP.NET Core / Express convention).
  Convenience, not new design: build()
  applies the layers in reverse.
end note

note as N1
  All three are the Decorator pattern.
  The tests assert they produce identical behavior:
  same supplier calls, same log lines, same metrics.
  Choose by what reads best for the concern at hand.
end note

@enduml
